CHANGES LOG
$Id: CHANGES,v 1.2 2004/03/29 23:12:04 antirez Exp $

------ phase 2: architecture and run time lifecycle (2026-09) -------

See docs/ASAMA2-RAPORU.md and docs/KARAR-KAYITLARI.md. In short:

  - State split into struct hping_config (cfg), struct hping_context
    (ctx) and struct hping_stats (stats), with init functions (globals.c).
  - One monotonic clock for RTT, intervals and deadlines (clock.c); the
    wall clock only for the ICMP timestamp option; clock and random
    sources are replaceable (tests).
  - Lifecycle init/run/stop/destroy and a poll(2) event loop
    (lifecycle.c): packets are no longer sent from a SIGALRM handler,
    SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGTSTP handlers only set a flag and wake the loop
    through a pipe; the capture handle runs in immediate, non-blocking
    mode.
  - No exit() below the command line entry points: parse_options()
    returns OK/DONE/ERROR, the send path returns status codes,
    hping_run() returns the exit status.
  - Statistics: 64 bit counters, duplicate and unmatched replies counted
    separately, packet loss = (sent - unique replies) * 100 / sent (like
    ping; 2 of 3 answered is now 33%, was 34%), "transmitted" spelling.
  - --scan runs in the single event loop (no fork, no SysV shared
    memory, which also leaked a segment per scan); port 65535 is scanned.
  - --apd-send is executed after parsing, unsupported ICMP types and bad
    --rand-dest templates are rejected before any socket is opened,
    usage errors go to stderr.
  - Behaviour changes worth knowing: -i 0 now sends as fast as possible
    (it used to stop after the first packet), --flood -c N sends exactly
    N packets, --count is satisfied by unique replies only.
  - Tests: tests/test_loop.c (fake clock, scripted replies, real signal
    wake up, savefile I/O), tests/test_parse.c, scan state machine tests,
    standalone libars.a link check; 723 checks in total.

------ phase 1: reliable build and critical fixes (2026-09) -------

See docs/ASAMA1-RAPORU.md for the full report. In short:

Build
  - configure rewritten: --prefix/--sbindir/--mandir/--datadir, --no-tcl
    is honoured before Tcl detection, --with-tcl makes a missing Tcl
    fatal, CC/CPPFLAGS/CFLAGS/LDFLAGS/LDLIBS/AR/RANLIB are honoured,
    libpcap and Tcl are found through pkg-config/tclConfig.sh and
    verified with a test program, failures exit with status 1.
  - Makefile: DESTDIR/prefix installation with relative symlinks,
    make check (offline test-suite), make WERROR=1, make SANITIZE=1.
  - net/bpf.h is only included where it exists (Linux builds again),
    delaytable is no longer defined in a header (GCC >= 10 -fno-common),
    strlcpy.c is skipped when the C library provides strlcpy.
  - byteorder.h prefers the compiler's __BYTE_ORDER__ (cross builds).

Memory safety / parsers (all covered by tests in tests/)
  - pcap_recv() copies at most the buffer size (frames larger than the
    buffer were written past it).
  - scan.c: ICMP header copy size, IHL/length sanity checks, port range
    bounds, "1-" syntax error, URG flag letter.
  - waitpacket.c: quoted headers in ICMP errors are checked before being
    copied, TCP option parsing terminates on zero-length options and
    respects the captured length, HCMP length check had its sign
    reversed, IHL < 5 is rejected.
  - display_ipopt(): option parsing is bounded by the header length.
  - send.c --rand-dest: octet buffers were one byte too small; octets
    are validated (0-255 or x).
  - memstr(): needle length no longer stored in a char; bounds fixed.
  - sendicmp.c: quoted IP/UDP headers copied with their real size.
  - ARS: split.c reads headers through aligned copies and checks the
    available size before every field access (truncated TCP/UDP headers,
    tot_len < IHL, IHL < 5, zero-length options, options without length
    byte); rapd.c never reads past the layer (option data, SACK blocks,
    unknown options); ars_cksum()/ars_multi_cksum() no longer read one
    byte past odd-sized buffers; ars_udptcp_cksum() with no IP layer
    no longer reads p_layer[-1]; the TCP option padding lost the
    realloc()ed pointer (use after free).
  - APD: hex data validation really rejects non-hex digits, tcp.ts has a
    working setter (val/ecr), tcp.timestamp and tcp.echoreq are accepted
    as aliases so that rapd output parses again, ip.sec tcc lands in the
    right field, tcp.wscale shift is no longer byte-swapped, rapd prints
    the IP checksum in host order like the other checksums.
  - Tcl: 'hping recv' with several packets described each packet from a
    wrong offset (and could overflow the buffer); read errors/end of
    savefile are reported instead of looping; 'hping event ifname {}'
    removes the file handler; Tcl_GetIntFromObj() errors are propagated;
    'hping recvraw' returns byte arrays and 'hping sendraw' accepts
    them; Tcl_Size compatibility typedef, no deprecated panic().
  - hex.c: the digit table was signed, invalid digits were accepted.
  - globals.h: ip_optlen declared with its real type (unsigned int).

Command line
  - --faster no longer also enables --tr-keep-ttl (missing break).
  - numeric option values are validated (whole argument consumed, range
    of the protocol field); bad values are rejected with a message and
    exit status 1 instead of being silently truncated.
  - help text and man page: --fast is -i u100000, --faster is -i u1,
    the default window is 512.

30Mar2004 - First public release of hping3
